SECTION 3 — OFF-HOST COPY FOUND BY VOLUME LABEL, NOT DRIVE LETTER
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DriveLetter     : D
FileSystemLabel : MERCHBACKUP
FileSystem      : exFAT
DriveType       : Removable
SizeGB          : 7.45

The configured value is the LABEL "MERCHBACKUP". The drive letter never
appears in configuration or code. A stick that mounts as E: or F: on a
classmate's machine is still found — which matters because the demo runs on
three machines nobody has surveyed (ADR-012, ADR-015).

ACCEPTED LIMITATION: exFAT carries no ACLs, so the dump on the stick is
readable by anyone who plugs it in, and a dump contains every password hash.
Reformatting to NTFS was considered and rejected: ACLs on removable media are
defeated by taking ownership on any machine with admin, so NTFS would buy the
appearance of protection rather than protection. The honest control is
physical custody of the stick, and that is what docs/installation-guide.md
now says. Encrypting the dump is the real answer if confidentiality ever
matters; for demo accounts with no real personnel data it is over-engineering.


SECTION 4 — APPEND-ONLY, ENFORCED BY GRANT (ADR-013, amended)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
merch_backup holds INSERT on exactly one table and no other write privilege
anywhere in the schema. Proven by attempting each forbidden operation as that
account against the real server:

--- UPDATE BackupLogs ---
ERROR 1142 (42000) at line 1: UPDATE command denied to user
'merch_backup'@'localhost' for table `merchandising`.`backuplogs`

--- DELETE BackupLogs ---
ERROR 1142 (42000) at line 1: DELETE command denied to user
'merch_backup'@'localhost' for table `merchandising`.`backuplogs`

--- INSERT into a DIFFERENT table (AuditLogs) ---
ERROR 1142 (42000) at line 1: INSERT command denied to user
'merch_backup'@'localhost' for table `merchandising`.`auditlogs`

The third one is the one worth reading twice: it proves the INSERT grant is
scoped to backuplogs alone, not widened across the schema. A failed backup
run therefore cannot be edited into a successful one, and the backup account
cannot write anywhere else even if the utility were compromised.

Grants as they now stand:

GRANT USAGE ON *.* TO `merch_backup`@`localhost`
GRANT SELECT, LOCK TABLES, SHOW VIEW, EVENT, TRIGGER ON `merchandising`.*
      TO `merch_backup`@`localhost`
GRANT INSERT ON `merchandising`.`backuplogs` TO `merch_backup`@`localhost`


